How do you prevent reports from parallel workers overwriting files?

Parallel execution creates concurrency problems when multiple workers write to the same screenshot, log, or report file.

Interview-ready answer:

“I generate a unique execution ID and a unique worker or test identifier, isolate mutable test state, and use report-tool integrations that support parallel execution. Each worker writes its evidence to a separate location, and a controlled final stage combines or publishes the results.”

A practical artifact structure:

artifacts/
  build-582/
    worker-0/
      login_failure_001.png
      test.log
    worker-1/
      login_failure_002.png
      test.log
    worker-2/
      transfer_failure_001.png
      test.log

A filename can include:

CI build or run ID

Worker ID

Test method name

Timestamp or UUID

Example:

String artifactName = System.getenv("BUILD_NUMBER")
        + "_worker-" + workerId
        + "_" + testName
        + "_" + java.util.UUID.randomUUID()
        + ".png";

Make sure workerId and testName are safely obtained and sanitized, and handle missing environment variables.

For reporting tools:

Allure: each worker writes results to an isolated allure-results directory; combine the result files before generating the final report. Avoid collisions in attachment filenames.

Extent Reports: use a supported parallel-execution strategy, keep test contexts isolated, and coordinate writes to shared report state. Don't assume a shared ExtentTest or report instance is automatically thread-safe.

TestNG: use thread-local or test-context-based WebDriver management. Never use one static mutable driver for unrelated parallel tests.

CI workers: upload each worker's artifacts, then merge or aggregate in a dedicated reporting stage.

Banking example: Two workers execute transfers simultaneously. If both tests share the same account and test data, the issue isn't only a report collision—it may also create false failures or financial-data contamination. Use isolated accounts or test entities, unique transaction references, and safe cleanup.

7. How do you prevent sensitive banking data appearing in screenshots and logs?

This is particularly important for an ADIB banking role.

Interview-ready answer:

“I apply data minimization and masking at the source. I use synthetic test data, avoid logging credentials or full account and card numbers, mask sensitive UI fields before capturing screenshots where feasible, restrict artifact access, and enforce retention policies. I also validate that the masking works rather than assuming it does.”

Practical controls
Use synthetic or approved test data. Never use real customer details just because they are available in a test environment.

Mask UI fields. Hide or redact account numbers, card numbers, balances, customer identifiers, and personal details before screenshot capture.

Sanitize logs. Don't print passwords, access tokens, session cookies, authorization headers, OTPs, or full financial identifiers.

Secure artifacts. Apply access controls, appropriate encryption, limited retention, and approved storage.

Protect API evidence. Redact sensitive request and response fields before attaching them to reports.

Control failure traces. Browser traces, HAR files, network logs, and console logs may contain more sensitive data than screenshots.

Verify masking. Include automated checks for known sensitive test values in generated logs and report artifacts.

A basic masking helper for logs:

public static String maskAccountNumber(String accountNumber) {
    if (accountNumber == null || accountNumber.length() <= 4) {
        return "****";
    }

    return "****" + accountNumber.substring(
            accountNumber.length() - 4);
}

Use it like this:

logger.info("Transfer initiated for account {}",
        maskAccountNumber(accountNumber));

This helper is only a basic example. It does not identify every sensitive field automatically; real sanitization must be applied consistently to structured logs, exception messages, API payloads, and reporting attachments.

Screenshot warning: Blurring an element through CSS may not be enough if the underlying page, browser trace, or DOM snapshot still contains the original value. Prefer approved synthetic data and verified redaction, and avoid collecting sensitive evidence in the first place.

Strong senior-level phrase: “A screenshot is a test artifact, but it can also be a data-leak channel. I treat screenshots, traces, API payloads, and logs as potentially sensitive.”    

4. How do you generate JUnit XML for Jenkins or Azure DevOps?

JUnit XML is a common test-result format that CI systems can parse to show test counts, failures, skipped tests, and execution details.

Java + Maven + TestNG

Add a compatible TestNG dependency and Maven Surefire plugin to your pom.xml. For example:

<build>
    <plugins>
        <plugin>
            <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
            <artifactId>maven-surefire-plugin</artifactId>
            <version>3.5.4</version>
            <configuration>
                <suiteXmlFiles>
                    <suiteXmlFile>testng.xml</suiteXmlFile>
                </suiteXmlFiles>
                <reportsDirectory>
                    ${project.build.directory}/surefire-reports
                </reportsDirectory>
            </configuration>
        </plugin>
    </plugins>
</build>

Run:

mvn clean test

The Surefire plugin typically generates TestNG-compatible XML files such as:

target/surefire-reports/
    TEST-com.company.tests.LoginTests.xml
    testng-results.xml

The exact files depend on the Maven, Surefire, and TestNG configuration. Confirm the generated output rather than assuming every filename exists.

Jenkins
post {
    always {
        junit(
            testResults: 'target/surefire-reports/TEST-*.xml',
            allowEmptyResults: false
        )
        archiveArtifacts(
            artifacts: 'target/surefire-reports/**',
            allowEmptyArchive: true
        )
    }
}

The junit step publishes the results to Jenkins. Archiving retains the XML and related files as build artifacts. If no XML is produced, the build should not silently report success.

Azure DevOps
- task: PublishTestResults@2
  displayName: Publish Test Results
  condition: succeededOrFailed()
  inputs:
    testResultsFormat: JUnit
    testResultsFiles: '**/TEST-*.xml'
    failTaskOnFailedTests: true
    failTaskOnMissingResultsFile: true

Important distinction: Generating XML and publishing XML are two different steps. Your framework generates the results; the CI publisher reads and displays them.

Real-world example: If 500 regression tests run and 3 fail, JUnit XML enables the CI dashboard to show the failed tests and their failure messages without requiring the reviewer to open every HTML report.

5. How do you publish reports when the pipeline fails?

Interview-ready answer:

“I separate test execution from report publication. I configure publication and artifact collection to run even when the test command fails, while preserving the original test failure as the pipeline result. I also fail the pipeline if the expected results are missing.”

Example Jenkins pipeline:

pipeline {
    agent any

    stages {
        stage('Run Tests') {
            steps {
                sh 'mvn clean test'
            }
        }
    }

    post {
        always {
            junit(
                testResults: 'target/surefire-reports/TEST-*.xml',
                allowEmptyResults: false
            )

            archiveArtifacts(
                artifacts: 'target/surefire-reports/**,target/failure-screenshots/**',
                allowEmptyArchive: true
            )
        }
    }
}

This is a simplified example. In a production pipeline, account for cases where the publisher 
itself fails, and ensure artifact collection doesn't accidentally mask the original test failure.
 If tests run in a Docker container or Kubernetes Job, copy the results out first.

For example:

docker cp qa-runner:/app/target/surefire-reports ./surefire-reports

Kubernetes example: If a Job's container exits with code 1, collect its logs and test-result files 
before deleting the Pod or Job. For long-running clusters, use an artifact store or persistent volume 
so evidence survives Pod cleanup.

Common trap: allowEmptyResults: true can conceal a broken reporting setup. Use it only when missing
 results are genuinely expected; for a required regression suite, missing results should normally fail 
 the quality gate.
 

